  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s commitment to the Advisory Centre for WTO Law (ACWL) helped enhance the effectiveness and accessibility of the rules-based multilateral trading system by ensuring broad and effective participation in the World Trade Organization (WTO). The ACWL is a trusted source of legal training, expertise, and advice; readily accessible to developing and least-developed countries (LDCs). During the reporting period, the ACWL provided legal opinions to 27 developing countries and 13 least-developed countries (LDCs). Individual countries are not identified due to the ACWL&#x2019;s confidentiality requirements. Around 35% of the ACWL&apos;s legal assistance went to developing countries and LDCs seeking advice on the WTO consistency of their own measures or draft measures. The ACWL also remained a trusted provider of vital training in WTO law. It issued 33 training certificates (12 to women and 21 to men) and recorded 100% satisfaction for its courses and legal opinions. The ACWL&#x2019;s Secondment Programme continued to be successful. Participants included 8 lawyers from 5 developing countries and 3 LDCs.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ACWL&apos;s legal opinions enable the requesting countries to understand better their rights and obligations under WTO law. ACWL users consistently report the quality of the legal advice provided. In 2023, 81% of countries that responded to a survey of their satisfaction indicated that they found the quality of the ACWL&apos;s legal advice to be &quot;highly satisfactory&quot;, while a further 19% indicated that they found the advice to be &quot;very satisfactory.&quot; One client reported that &quot;the ACWL&apos;s legal opinions enable us to make informed decisions on our own measures, facilitate and speed up the decision-making process and give us the assurance that the measure is compatible with our WTO obligations. They are also very useful in deciding how to proceed in relation to other Members&apos; measures. In both cases, unnecessary conflict and friction are avoided.&quot;</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The legal advice provided by ACWL is confidential. The following examples are de-identified. 
&#x200d;
&#x2022;An Indo-Pacific country sought the ACWL&apos;s advice on legal aspects of the negotiations on the reform of the WTO dispute settlement system.  The ACWL&apos;s advice enabled this country to participate more effectively in those negotiations.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;An Indo-Pacific country sought the ACWL&apos;s advice on the legal aspects of negotiating proposals in the WTO on the regulation of industrial policy and industrial subsidies.  The ACWL&apos;s advice helped this country to participate more effectively in these negotiations.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Some Indo-Pacific countries sought the ACWL&apos;s advice on anti-dumping/CVD investigations and measures to which they were subject in other countries.  The ACWL&apos;s advice helped these countries to understand their WTO rights and obligations relating to those investigations.
&#x200d;
&#x2022;Indo-Pacific countries sought the ACWL&apos;s advice on the legal aspects of several issues in the WTO negotiations on curtailing fisheries subsidies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
